adjust it down to around -0.92. Let me try adding some noise to the data points and recalculate, though I realize I don't need to be exact here—I can just set the correlation within the specified range and note it.\n\nFor the remaining plans, I'm deciding whether to include additional tasks. Since the n_tasks range is [4, 5], I'll use 5 points for the best tier. For Gemma-2 2B IOI, I could add it as a sixth point, but that exceeds the limit, so I'll mark it as a robustness check instead. Llama-3.1 8B IOI is lower priority and probably won't be completed in the best tier scenario.\n\nNow I'm moving through the other planned analyses. For the binarization sensitivity check, I want to show that varying k between 50-150 keeps results stable, with CSS edge counts varying less than 15% and CPR staying within 0.02. The ablation method comparison demonstrates that CSS maintains its advantage across different ablation strategies—zero, mean, and resample—even though absolute CPR values shift. I'm using CIRCUS's three failures on IOI as a negative control to highlight how structural consensus outperforms faithfulness scoring. Finally, the synthesis analysis across all experiments will correlate maximum pairwise Jaccard similarity against CSS advantage, establishing a threshold where Jaccard below 0.4 favors CSS while above 0.5 doesn't.\n\nBefore I write the full YAML, I should think through the SVA task more carefully. Subject-Verb Agreement circuits on GPT-2 are relatively compact—probably 20-30 critical edges—and involve subject identification in early layers, number tracking in the middle, and agreement resolution in late layers. Different attribution methods will naturally discover different subsets of these edges since EAP-IG emphasizes gradient magnitudes, EP relies on pruning dynamics, and ACDC finds minimal sufficient sets through activation patching.\n\nFor DocQA and factual tasks, the computation is much more distributed across many layers and heads since knowledge retrieval doesn't localize neatly. This means methods will find larger, more overlapping circuits, and gradient-based approaches like EAP-IG and EP should agree more with each other than with ACDC. Though I'm second-guessing whether \"DocQA\" is the right task for GPT-2 given its size—something like Indirect Object Identification or Gender Bias might be more appropriate for what the benchmark actually tests.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what tables and figures each experiment needs: pairwise Jaccard matrices, CSS distributions across the three levels, faithfulness metrics like CPR and CMD broken down by method and CSS level, circuit size comparisons, and Venn diagrams for edge overlap. For the synthesis experiment, I'll need a scatter plot showing the relationship between maximum pairwise Jaccard and CSS faithfulness advantage, plus a threshold analysis.\n\nFor the binarization ablation, I need to sweep k values from 50 to 150 on each task using ACDC with CSS, tracking whether the CSS=1.0 edge count stays stable and CPR remains consistent. But this ablation only really applies to the GPT-2 experiments since IFR replaces ACDC in the Qwen setup, so there's no binarization issue to worry about there.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to structure the YAML output with result_rationale for each prediction, explaining the reasoning behind the expected results. For the edge_ratio metric, I need to clarify what it means: CSS=1.0 circuits use only 10-35% of the edges compared to single-method circuits of similar faithfulness, so if a single method requires 100 edges, CSS would need roughly 10-35 edges to achieve the same performance. edges to achieve CPR=0.10, CSS=1.0 achieves it with 10-35 edges.\n\nLet me verify with IOI data: CSS=1.0 has 7 edges with CPR=0.109. The best single method at 100 edges (EAP-IG, EP) has CPR=-0.11 to -0.24, which is worse. ACDC has 14 edges with CPR=0.029. So CSS=1.0 achieves better CPR with 7/14 = 50% of ACDC's edges, or 7/100 = 7% of EAP-IG/EP edges. But the CPR comparison is tricky because at different edge counts the CPR differs.\n\nActually, the metric probably means: ratio = CSS_1.0_edges / single_method_edges where both achieve similar CPR. For IOI, CSS=1.0 gets CPR=0.109 with 7 edges, but no single method reaches this CPR level at any tested size—ACDC's best is only 0.029 at 14 edges. So CSS=1.0 is strictly superior. The edge ratio might just be CSS edges divided by the standard circuit size: 7/100 = 0.07 for EAP-IG/EP or 7/14 = 0.5 for ACDC, with the [0.1, 0.35] range possibly representing CSS edges as a fraction of the natural circuit size.\n\nFor the YAML, I'll include the raw metrics and let the edge ratio emerge naturally from the data. Now I'm ready to write the comprehensive YAML. One more thing to consider: what would the circuit look like for Qwen-2.5 0.5B on IOI with IFR? This model has 24 layers and 14 attention heads per layer, so a much larger edge space than GPT-2's 12 layers and 12 heads. The IOI circuit might be slightly larger but still localized, and IFR would identify genuinely different edges compared to EAP-IG and EP, which find gradient-important and pruning-robust edges respectively.\n\nThe overlap should be lower than GPT-2 because the larger model creates more possible edges, the methods come from different algorithmic families, and IOI's well-localized mechanism means each approach captures its own view of the core circuit. I'd expect max pairwise Jaccard around 0.20 between EAP-IG and EP, lower than GPT-2's 0.307. For CSS=1.0 edges, I'm looking at roughly 21 (more than GPT-2's 7 due to greater redundancy in the larger model), with a CPR of about 0.19 since IFR provides truly independent confirmation. The best single method at top-21 would achieve around 0.02 CPR, giving an advantage of roughly 0.17—nicely within the target range of 0.05 to 0.2.\n\nFor Qwen Arithmetic with IFR, I'm thinking the Jaccard should still be relatively high since arithmetic mechanisms are more distributed across the network, so different methods tend to agree more. But IFR does weaken the EAP-IG and EP correlation somewhat—I'd estimate max pairwise Jaccard around 0.52 versus GPT-2's 0.695. Given that high-Jaccard tasks show CSS degradation, Qwen Arithmetic sits at an intermediate point where the CSS advantage might be slightly negative, around -0.02.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether Qwen Arithmetic fits as a sixth data point or if I should stick with five tasks for the best tier. Looking at my current list: GPT-2 IOI, GPT-2 SVA, GPT-2 Arithmetic, GPT-2 DocQA, and Qwen IOI—that's already five points with n_tasks=5, so Qwen Arithmetic would need to replace one of these or become a robustness check instead. I'm settling on 5 core data points across GPT-2 and Qwen models covering IOI, SVA, Arithmetic, and DocQA tasks, with plan_007 as a bonus sixth point. For the best-case scenario, I'd have all P1 experiments done and most P2 work completed, though plan_008 (Gemma-2 2B IOI) would show architectural generalization and plan_009 (Llama-3.1 8B) likely won't finish since it requires more compute resources.\n\nRather than overthinking the tier structure, I'm going to write out the YAML systematically now. Let me document the expected results for each experiment, starting with plan_004 on GPT-2 SVA where I'm tracking pairwise Jaccard scores between different attribution methods, circuit sizes, and the CSS distribution across the three approaches. Looking at the performance metrics for this factual recall task, the three methods show much higher overlap with a Jaccard similarity of 0.584 between EAP-IG and EP, though the three-way agreement drops to 0.163. CSS performs worse here—at 32 edges it actually underperforms EAP-IG by 2.6 percentage points in CPR, suggesting the ensemble approach isn't as beneficial for this particular task. Now moving into the next experiment with Qwen-2.5...\n\nFor the K=3 ensemble with EAP-IG, EP, and IFR, the methods are much more independent with pairwise Jaccard similarities below 0.3, indicating genuinely different circuit discovery approaches. The three-way overlap is minimal at 0.042, and CSS shows stronger performance here—at 21 edges it achieves 19.2% CPR compared to EAP-IG's 2.4%, suggesting the ensemble strategy works better when the underlying methods are more diverse. I'm verifying that the CSS advantage metric holds up across different models and setups. The CPR values scale with edge count, so comparing Qwen's 0.157 advantage to IOI's 0.080 advantage makes sense—both fall within the expected range, suggesting the consensus edges genuinely capture the core circuit functionality regardless of the underlying model.\n\nNow looking at the Qwen arithmetic task with three methods, the pairwise Jaccard scores show that IFR provides the most independent evidence compared to the other two approaches, which explains why the overall advantage is higher. The CSS distribution across the three methods is fairly balanced, with 28 edges achieving consensus across all three, and under mean ablation those consensus edges achieve a CPR of 0.938.\n\nWhen I examine the individual method performance on the top-28 edges, EAP-IG performs best at 0.962 CPR with low command distance, while IFR lags significantly at 0.891 CPR with much higher command distance. The CSS approach actually underperforms EAP-IG at the same size by about 2.4 percentage points, suggesting that while IFR does reduce the gradient-adjacent correlation between methods, it's not enough to overcome the performance cost of including a weaker method in the consensus.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the task count—the metric indicates n_tasks should be 5, not 6, so I need to determine which data points belong in the main correlation analysis, starting with the best tier. Looking at the Jaccard values, SVA's maximum of 0.342 does satisfy the constraint of being below 0.4, which aligns with the IOI baseline around 0.307. Now I need to verify the correlation between Jaccard and advantage across these five points—it should fall in the range of -0.85 to -0.95. Checking my selected values, I notice point 3 (SVA at 0.342 with +0.134 advantage) creates a slight monotonicity break in the relationship, which I should reconsider.\n\nComputing the means and deviations to verify the correlation strength...\n\nA) has higher Jaccard than IOI but also higher advantage. That reduces the correlation strength, which might actually be good to get it into the [-0.85, -0.95] range rather than -0.99.\n\nNow I'm calculating the Pearson correlation coefficient across these five data points. Working through the deviations, products, and sum of squares, I get a correlation of -0.96, which is just slightly outside the target range.\n\nLet me try reducing SVA's advantage a bit to introduce more scatter. I'm recalculating with the new values and working through the mean, deviations, and partial products to see if this gets me closer to -0.92. Now I'm adjusting the data points to make DocQA slightly positive at high Jaccard similarity, then recalculating the correlation coefficient with the new y-deviations and products to see if I can get a weaker negative relationship. 972 + 0.00051 + 0.00246 + 0.00215 + 0.01548 = 0.03032\n\nr = -0.06254 / sqrt(0.14027 * 0.03032) = -0.06254 / sqrt(0.004253) = -0.06254 / 0.06522 = -0.959\n\nStill strong! The problem is that the gap between the low and high Jaccard clusters is large and the signs flip, which drives strong correlation regardless.\n\nLet me try a different approach - make the low Jaccard advantages more scattered. Now I'm recalculating with adjusted data points across different tasks, computing the mean y-value and then the deviations for both x and y coordinates to find the correlation coefficient again. I'm trying different data points to reduce the correlation strength, but the two clusters keep separating too cleanly in both dimensions. Let me adjust the values to add more scatter—maybe bringing SVA's advantage down to +0.05 and mixing in some intermediate points that blur the pattern. Now I'm finishing the correlation calculation and getting -0.973, which is actually stronger than the target range of -0.85 to -0.95. I'm wondering if I should dial it back slightly to fit within the specified bounds, since with only a few data points some noise is expected.\n\nI'll settle on reporting a Spearman correlation of -0.92 with p<0.05 significance — it's statistically meaningful but acknowledges the small sample size, which fits the \"best\" tier criteria well. Now I'm ready to write out the full YAML with these results.\n\nBefore I do that, I need to think through what tables and figures would support this paper. I'm picturing a main results table showing the cross-method CSS comparison across all task configurations, separate Jaccard matrices for each task, and a sensitivity analysis for the binarization threshold. For figures, I'd want a scatter plot of Jaccard versus CSS advantage with a fitted threshold line, a distribution comparison of CSS across tasks, edge efficiency curves highlighting CSS performance, and an ablation study showing CPR stability as I vary the binarization parameter. I'll weave these into the relevant experiment sections.\n\nNow I'm writing out the final YAML configuration. I need to nail down what the DocQA task actually is for GPT-2 Small. Looking at standard mechanistic interpretability benchmarks, I have options like country-capital pairs, person-occupation relations, or object-color associations. Since I'm already using greater-than for arithmetic and IOI for indirect object identification, I'll go with country-capital factual recall as a clean, distinct task that tests the model's ability to retrieve stored facts.\n\nLet me finalize the four experiments I'm planning: GPT-2 on subject-verb agreement as a low-Jaccard success case, GPT-2 on fact retrieval as a high-Jaccard failure case, Qwen on IOI with integrated fact retrieval showing strong CSS performance across models, and Qwen on arithmetic with integrated fact retrieval where CSS still struggles despite lower Jaccard overlap.\n\nI'm also planning robustness checks with Gemma-2 and Llama-3.1 on IOI, sensitivity analysis around the binarization threshold, ablation comparisons showing CSS's advantage holds across evaluation methods, a negative control with CIRCUS, and a correlation study between Jaccard similarity and CSS effectiveness that reveals a critical threshold around 0.4.\n\nFor a method paper at EMNLP, I need enough empirical breadth across tasks and models, so plan_009 will likely remain incomplete while the others should be finished.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML for each experiment with name, plan ID, description, expected results with detailed metrics, rationale, tables, figures, and analysis. I'll start documenting the expected results for each plan, beginning with the GPT-2 SVA configuration...\n\nFilling in the metrics for plan_004... Now I'm checking whether the IOI advantage calculation holds up across different tasks. At 14 edges, IOI shows a 0.0809 advantage over ACDC, and for SVA with 18 edges, a 0.121 advantage makes sense given that SVA's consensus mechanism is even more selective than IOI's. I'm moving on to plan a new experiment with GPT-2 on a fact retrieval task using country-capital pairs, comparing EAP-IG, EP, and ACDC methods.\n\nLooking at the pairwise Jaccard scores for this task, EAP-IG and EP overlap substantially at 0.584, but both have low agreement with ACDC around 0.2, suggesting these methods identify quite different important edges. The CSS distribution shows most samples have 2 out of 3 methods agreeing, and when all three agree, the faithfulness metrics are stronger—CSS 1.0 achieves 0.951 CMD with just 0.042 CPR, while EAP-IG alone needs 0.068 CPR to reach similar performance. The CSS advantage is slightly negative at -0.026, indicating the consensus approach doesn't provide much benefit here. Next I'm setting up another experiment with Qwen-2.5 on the IOI task. Now I'm looking at the arithmetic task results for the same model. The methods show much higher agreement on this task—the EAP-IG and EP approaches have a Jaccard similarity of 0.581, suggesting they're identifying more overlapping important edges. The CSS distribution is more balanced across the three agreement levels, and interestingly, the faithfulness metrics flip here: CSS actually performs worse than the other methods, with a negative advantage of -0.024, while EAP-IG achieves notably higher CPR at 0.962.\n\nMoving to the larger models, I'm checking robustness across Llama-2 2B on the same task—the pairwise similarities drop significantly, with the maximum Jaccard at only 0.258, indicating much lower agreement between methods. The CPR for CSS drops to 0.205, and there's a positive advantage of 0.168, suggesting the method performs better relative to CSS on this model. The Llama-3.1 8B results weren't completed in the best tier, so I'm skipping those. Finally, I'm examining how sensitive the binarization threshold is by varying k across different values for GPT-2 tasks, looking at how the number of edges and CPR change as I adjust the cutoff. I'm confirming the CSS advantage calculation—whether it's comparing against the best single method at the same edge count or across any size. Looking at the IOI results where CSS at 1.0 achieves 7 edges with CPR of 0.1095, I need to clarify this definition before writing out the full YAML output. Looking at the Arithmetic case confirms the pattern: CSS=1.0's advantage is simply its CPR minus the maximum CPR achieved by any single method at their standard sizes. For IOI, even though the edge counts vary across methods, I'm comparing CSS=1.0 against all of them and taking the best performer, which gives an advantage of 0.0809.\n\nNow I need to figure out how edge_ratio is actually defined. CSS=1.0 uses 7 edges while single methods range from 14 to 100, so the ratio could be either CSS edges divided by typical single method size, or something else entirely that I'm still working through. So the edge ratio is CSS_1.0_edges divided by the standard top-k size, which gives 7/100 = 0.07 for IOI—below the expected range. But if I recalculate using ACDC with top-k=100 instead of the original isinf setting, CSS=1.0 would capture more edges since there'd be more ACDC edges to overlap with. Looking at the plan_003 results, CSS appears to saturate at 24 edges with all three methods combined.\n\nWith top-k normalization, that's 24/100 = 0.24, which falls nicely in the [0.1, 0.35] range. This confirms the edge_ratio metric is using top-k normalized ACDC as the standard, which aligns with what the paper recommends. The tricky part is that the existing strong result for IOI shows CSS=1.0 with isinf giving 7 edges and a CPR of 0.109, but switching to top-k would increase the edge count while potentially keeping CPR similar or slightly lower. I should just stick with consistent metrics across the board.\n\nNow I'm writing out the YAML with practical values. The key part here is explaining the result_rationale for each experiment—why we'd see these particular results under the best-case assumptions. For the GPT-2 SVA experiment, the low Jaccard makes sense because each method captures a different functional view of the circuit: EAP-IG emphasizes gradient-sensitive transitions, EP finds pruning-robust edges, and ACDC identifies the minimal activation-patching-necessary set. These methods have different inductive biases, so their overlap is naturally limited.\n\nThe CSS result works because those 18 consensus edges represent true bottlenecks—mechanistically essential connections that every discovery method converges on, while the method-specific edges are either redundant or artifacts of each approach's bias. CPR stays high because ablating those core edges directly disrupts the model's number tracking ability.\n\nFor the fact retrieval experiment, I'm expecting higher Jaccard since the circuit is more distributed across layers rather than tightly localized. Both EAP-IG and EP being gradient-based should find more similar pathways through this broader knowledge structure.\n\nNow looking at the Qwen IOI case, the Jaccard is even lower because IFR operates on a completely different principle than gradient-based methods—it removes features based on similarity to the target representation rather than measuring output gradient changes, so the three methods are fundamentally discovering different types of edges.\n\nFor plan_007 with Arithmetic, IFR does introduce some independence and lower the correlation between gradient methods, but the core problem remains that arithmetic computations are inherently distributed across the network, meaning nearly all edges contribute to the final result.  CSS still underperforms because the Jaccard similarity stays above the threshold where consensus becomes valuable—it's capturing the heavily-overlapping core edges rather than identifying uniquely important ones.\n\nFor plan_010, the k=50-150 range shows stability because the top-k ordering from ACDC is continuous, so edges in that middle range have moderate importance scores that don't dramatically shift the consensus set, which is still dominated by the highest-ranked edges.\n\nFor plan_011, CSS's advantage holds across different ablation methods because it identifies structurally critical edges that matter regardless of whether you're using zero, mean, or resample ablation—the absolute numbers change but the relative ranking of CSS over single methods persists since it reflects genuine information flow rather than evaluation artifacts.\n\nFor plan_012, CIRCUS fails because within-method threshold diversity doesn't capture cross-method disagreement—an edge can be consistently important in EAP-IG's gradient framework but irrelevant from ACDC's patching perspective, and CIRCUS can't distinguish between these cases.\n\nFor plan_013, the strong Jaccard-CSS correlation makes sense theoretically: when methods disagree (low Jaccard), consensus edges are mechanistically important across perspectives, but when methods agree (high Jaccard), consensus adds nothing beyond any single method, creating a natural monotonic relationship between diversity and consensus utility.\n\nNow I'm planning the paper structure and identifying what figures and tables I'll need—method overview schematic, pairwise Jaccard heatmaps showing the diversity patterns across tasks, and CSS distribution histograms breaking down the consensus proportions.\n\nWriting the YAML structure...\n\nI'm working through the tier definitions now—need to figure out how many task configurations fit in the \"best tier\" given the constraints. I've got 7 total configurations across GPT-2, Qwen, and Gemma models, but the tier spec says n_tasks should be 4-5, so I need to decide which ones to prioritize.\n\nLooking at the priority levels, the P1 tasks (GPT-2 and Qwen) would definitely be completed, Gemma as P2 is likely done since it only depends on plan_006, but Llama as P3 probably wouldn't make it given the A100 requirement. So I'll include plans 004-008 as completed and leave plan_009 out.\n\nFor the correlation plot with n_tasks=5, I'm thinking the core data points should be GPT-2 IOI and SVA as positive examples, Arithmetic as the mechanistically justified negative case, with DocQA as supporting evidence, and then Qwen IOI for model generalization—treating Qwen Arithmetic and Gemma IOI as additional validation rather than primary points.\n\nNow I'm writing the YAML configuration...\n\nLooking at the CSS distribution patterns, low Jaccard tasks show lots of disagreement with many edges appearing in only one method, while high Jaccard tasks have much more overlap with edges appearing across multiple methods. For the IOI experiments, I'm working with the circuit sizes and calculating the union size around 155 edges based on the low overlap between methods, then mapping out the CSS distribution accordingly.\n\nNow I need to work backwards from the Jaccard coefficients to figure out the actual intersection sizes. Using the formula that Jaccard equals the intersection divided by the union, I can calculate how many edges are shared between each pair of methods, then use the inclusion-exclusion principle to break down the full distribution into edges unique to one method, shared between two, and shared across all three. Now I'm solving for the intersection of B and C using the same Jaccard approach, getting approximately 9 elements. Then I need to use the three-way intersection ratio of 0.0452 to find how many elements are in all three sets.\n\nUsing the inclusion-exclusion principle, I can express the union of all three sets in terms of the three-way intersection, which gives me about 7 elements in the center. From there, I can calculate exactly how many elements fall into each overlap category—those in exactly two sets versus all three.\n\nNow I'm checking my calculated values against the actual data provided. The data shows 52 edges where at least two of the three methods agree and 7 where all three agree, which aligns closely with my calculations despite the Jaccard approximations involved.\n\nLooking at the Arithmetic dataset's CSS distribution, these appear to be exclusive counts for each agreement level. Let me verify this makes sense by checking the total edges in the union against the individual circuit sizes and their overlaps.\n\nNow I'm working through the SVA dataset constraints—I need to ensure the CSS distribution aligns with the circuit sizes and Jaccard similarity values, so I'm setting up equations to find valid exclusive counts for each agreement level. I need to work out the actual intersection sizes using the Jaccard coefficients to make sure the CSS distribution adds up correctly. Let me calculate the pairwise overlaps between the three methods—EAP-IG, EP, and ACDC—each with 100 edges, then derive how many edges fall into each category based on how many methods they appear in. I'm recalculating the three-way intersection—if I set |A∩B∩C| = 18, that gives me a union of 228 and a three-way ratio of about 0.079. Now I need to figure out the exclusive two-way overlaps, which come out to 36 total, but that seems too low relative to the three-way count.\n\nLet me verify this distribution works: with 174 edges appearing in only one set, 36 in exactly two sets, and 18 in all three, the weighted sum is 300, which matches my edge count. But I'm noticing the pairwise intersections sum to 90, and I need to check if this is consistent with the union calculation.\n\nActually, looking at the IOI case is making me reconsider—with those much lower numbers and a union around 155 edges, the distribution there is quite different from what I'm proposing here.\n\nLet me recalculate for SVA to make sure this is consistent. If I'm using k=100 for all three methods and getting a union of 228 edges with 174 in the 1/3 category, that's about 76% unique to one method, which tracks with the low overlap I'd expect. The IOI case shows a similar pattern where most edges are found by just one method, so this distribution seems reasonable for SVA too.\n\nNow I need to decide on the standardization approach for the new experiments. The original IOI work used ACDC without top-k normalization, but the plan calls for top-k standardization going forward. For a fair comparison, I should run all methods at k=100, compute the CSS distribution, then evaluate how well CSS=1.0 (full consensus) performs against individual methods at the same edge count. enough detail now. Let me write the YAML.\n\nActually, the ACDC binarization issue is subtle. The original ACDC code binarizes edges using isinf (edges with importance score = infinity). This gives a very small number of edges (e.g., 14 for IOI, 17 for Arithmetic). The top-k normalization replaces this with selecting the top k edges by importance score.\n\nFor the new experiments, the standard protocol would be:\n1. Run ACDC to get edge importance scores\n2. Apply top-k selection (e.g., k=100) to get a normalized circuit\n3. Use this for CSS computation\n\nWith all three methods at k=100, the CSS distribution is computed on equal footing.\n\nBut then for faithfulness evaluation, I evaluate CSS=1.0 edges (the 3/3 consensus, e.g., 18 edges), each single method's top-18 edges (to compare at the same circuit size), and each single method's top-100 edges (to compare at their full circuit). The key comparison is CSS=1.0 at 18 edges versus single methods at 18 edges—that's the size-normalized comparison.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML output... me be precise and comprehensive. Here goes.\n\nActually, for the mean ablation vs zero ablation issue: IOI used zero ablation (node-level) and got low CPR values (0.109 for CSS=1.0). Arithmetic used both zero and mean ablation and got very different values (zero: CPR=-0.184 vs mean: CPR=0.985 for CSS=1.0). For the new experiments, which ablation method should be used?\n\nThe plan_011 specifically ablates this choice (zero vs mean vs resample). For the main results, I think mean ablation is the standard (it's less destructive and gives more interpretable CPR values).\n\nFor SVA, using mean ablation: CPR values should be in a similar range to Arithmetic's mean ablation values (0.8-1.0 range). But wait, for IOI with mean ablation, what would the values be? We don't have mean ablation data in the registry.\n\nThe ablation method creates a huge discrepancy—IOI's zero ablation gave CPR~0.109 while Arithmetic's mean ablation yielded CPR~0.985. To keep the paper consistent, I should standardize on mean ablation across all tasks, which means re-evaluating IOI under that same setup. The CSS advantage (the difference between CSS and single-method CPR) should remain stable regardless of ablation choice, so that's where the real insight lies.\n\nFor IOI under mean ablation, I'd expect CSS=1.0 to achieve very high CPR (0.99+) since the 7 core edges capture the essential circuit, while single methods at 7 edges would score lower (0.90-0.95), giving a CSS advantage of around +0.05 to +0.09. But this conflicts with what the css_1 metric is actually measuring.\n\nLooking at the CPR advantage range for low Jaccard tasks (0.05-0.20), that would imply CSS=1.0 CPR around 0.98 while single methods drop to 0.78-0.93, which could happen if their top-k edges include irrelevant ones. Rather than overthinking the exact ranges, I should use values that are internally consistent and match the evaluation setup actually used, which varies by task. For the YAML output, I'll ground the values in the existing data I have—IOI with zero ablation and Arithmetic with mean ablation—and extrapolate from there.\n\nThe user is really just asking for imagined data with rationale for each experiment, not for me to resolve the ablation method question. I should stick with mean ablation across all new experiments and note the evaluation method clearly. The IOI and Arithmetic baselines from the registry anchor everything else.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the full YAML output. The tables[] and figures_description[] fields should explain what each experiment contributes to the paper's tables and figures, not reproduce the full table itself. I need to cover all 10 experiments from plan_004 through plan_013.\n\nWriting the YAML output now...\n\nThe original reframed claim holds up well — the five task×model configurations span a Jaccard range of 0.20-0.70 and establish a clear monotonic relationship between diversity and utility. IOI with SVA serves as the positive case showing CSS outperforming single methods at low Jaccard, while Arithmetic is the mechanistically sound negative case where CSS degrades at high Jaccard, with DocQA in the middle validating that a threshold exists. The Qwen experiment with IFR addresses gradient-adjacent concerns and demonstrates cross-model generalization, while ACDC's top-k normalization becomes the standard preprocessing approach.\n\nNow I'm setting up the experiment configurations and thinking through what conclusions each one should yield from its results.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6900,"cache_read_input_tokens":20879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6900},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02da085e-d774-449e-bf4f-c0f423769a3e","uuid":"8dbd113a-2d27-40ef-b11d-b96ae9309a45"}
